    "handle": "AfricaisHOME2",
    "name": "AFRICA IS HOME GLOBAL",
    "text": "Airbus has identified system faults on an Air India A320 family jet after it experienced an uncommanded and limited pitch-down event in flight. The aircraft's autopilot remained engaged throughout, with only a brief and small loss of altitude, and the rest of the flight continued normally. A preliminary technical assessment pointed to a malfunction in the Elevator and Aileron Computer, or ELAC, as a possible contributing factor. Airbus said analysis of the event showed intense solar radiation may corrupt data critical to flight controls, which could lead in a worst case to uncommanded elevator movement and potentially exceed the aircraft's structural limits.\n\nIn response, Airbus and EASA issued an emergency airworthiness directive asking operators to carry out software and or hardware protection measures immediately. About 6,000 A320 family aircraft worldwide may be affected, roughly half the global fleet. Newer jets can get a software update in around 30 minutes, while older models need additional hardware changes that take longer. Air India said the directive will require a software or hardware realignment on part of its fleet of 120 to 125 A320s, leading to longer turnaround times and possible delays. IndiGo and Air India Express issued similar advisories. The airlines told passengers to expect some schedule adjustments over the weekend but said safety is the priority and no flights have been cancelled yet.\n\n- World Business News.",
    "createdAt": "2026-08-14T23:30:16Z",
