    "text": "Airbus is studying a potential stretch of its largest widebody aircraft that could directly challenge Boeing’s biggest twin-engine jet.\n\nThe proposed aircraft, often referred to as the A350-2000, would be a longer version of the Airbus A350-1000 and is being evaluated as a rival to the larger Boeing 777-9.\n\nAirbus executives have confirmed the concept is under internal study after airlines expressed interest in a bigger aircraft capable of carrying more passengers on busy long-haul routes.\n\nFormer Airbus commercial chief Christian Scherer said the company is actively examining the possibility of stretching the A350-1000 fuselage.\n\nThe idea would be relatively straightforward. Airbus could add around four to five additional rows, increasing capacity by roughly 40 to 45 seats while keeping the existing wings, landing gear and Rolls-Royce Trent XWB engines.\n\nThat would place the aircraft in the same size category as Boeing’s 777-9, which is designed to carry around 400 passengers.\n\nThe decision ultimately depends on airline demand. Airbus is already selling the A350 successfully, meaning a larger variant would only move forward if customers commit to significant orders.\n\nIf launched, the aircraft would mark Airbus’ most direct challenge yet to Boeing’s next-generation widebody.",
