    "name": "KC-10 Driver ✈️ 👨‍✈️ B-737 Wrangler",
    "text": "This is a recent development on the A320/321 NEO family w/ PW engines…Airbus just advised airlines of the issue & put some restrictions in place.\n\nTakeoffs are now prohibited in conditions of freezing fog when visibility is less than 150m (490’). This is due to the engine fan blades accumulating ice.\n\nThey also reduced the duration between “ice shedding” procedures on the ground. Basically, as you taxi you need to stop periodically, set the brakes & run the engines up to 60%. You let it stay there for a few seconds, then can pull it back. This spins the ice off the blades before so much of it can accumulate to damage the engine when you throw it off. Ice shedding is not limited to Airbus…most jet aircraft have some form of this procedure.\n\nI don’t think you get the freezing fog conditions that often, but it does happen & will cause a little pain.",
