    "text": "Hawaiian Airlines will end nonstop flights between Honolulu and Boston on November 19, closing the chapter on the nation’s longest current domestic route. The 4,427-nautical-mile service, launched in 2019, has been operated with Airbus A330-200 aircraft, offering four to five weekly departures.\n\nThe decision follows Delta’s withdrawal from the same market in April, leaving Hawaiian with only one East Coast destination, New York JFK. US Department of Transportation data shows the Boston route carried around 150,000 passengers annually but delivered the lowest revenue per seat mile of Hawaiian’s 16 mainland routes. The long stage length and relatively modest yields made it one of the carrier’s most expensive US operations.\n\nOnce service ends, Hawaiian plans to route Boston-bound travelers through Seattle in partnership with Alaska Airlines. The move also frees widebody capacity for other routes. With both Hawaiian and Delta gone, Boston will lose direct service to Honolulu for the first time in years.",
