    "name": "Flightdrama",
    "text": "Airlines across Southeast Asia are starting to take a closer look at China’s COMAC C919. With Airbus and Boeing both facing long delays and growing backlogs, some carriers now see the Chinese jet as a serious option.\n\nMalaysia’s transport minister, Anthony Loke, recently said that AirAsia and the new airline Air Borneo have shown interest. Neither has placed an order, but both are exploring the possibility as regional traffic to China keeps rising. Right now, over 1,000 flights run weekly between the two countries.\n\nThe C919 has a clear advantage. It is cheaper, easier to get, and fits the short-haul routes that dominate this region. For budget airlines trying to grow fast, that matters more than European certification, which the jet still doesn’t have.\n\nCOMAC has already made progress elsewhere. Laos took two C919s. VietJet is flying the ARJ21, a smaller jet built by the same company. Garuda Indonesia is in talks too.\n\nAirAsia is still reviewing the aircraft, and Air Borneo is doing the same. If either one moves forward, Malaysia’s aviation regulator would need to approve it.",
